## CONVENTION vs CONTENT — the distinction the whole run turns on
A first pass by other agents on this same corpus was **wrong about 40% of the time**, always in the same direction: calling a house convention a content bug. A false positive costs a human an hour. Be rigorous, not trigger-happy.

**NOT unfaithful — classify `CONVENTION_ONLY`:**
{{conventions_per_repo}}
- Any repo: {{conventions_shared}}

**CONTENT_DELTA — a real finding:**
different hypotheses (missing, extra, weaker, stronger); generality that does not specialise correctly (`ℝ` vs `ℝᵈ` where the book fixes one; closed vs half-open where it matters); a weaker or different conclusion; different quantifier scope (`∀ε∃N` vs `∃N∀ε`); wrong endpoint or normalisation convention; a vacuous or trivially-true statement; a "definition" that is not the book's definition; a wrong page anchor.

**Proxy definitions** deserve special attention. A bridge to a Mathlib object is SAFE when it is a *proved equivalence* recovering the book's text, and UNSAFE when the library object is adopted by definitional fiat. If a book Definition is only ever bridged and never stated, that is a `CONTENT` finding.

